%[text] %[text:anchor:T_555b] # Supervised Learning - Week 3 (Part 4)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_238a] ## Introduction
%[text] So far we've seen two major regression algorithms; **linear regression** and **logistic regression** for curve fitting tasks. In real world applications those algorithms might go through an issue which causes models perform poorly. In this part we will investigate one of those issues that our model can come across named <u>overfitting</u>.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4167] ## What is Overfitting?
%[text] In order to understand the concept of overfitting let's take a look at some regression examples. Let's go back to our original problem of predicting housing prices using linear regression, where the aim is to predict the price of a property by using its size.
rng(11);
co = colororder;

% Synthetic house size vs. prices data
lim_house_size = [-5 5];
house_size = linspace(min(lim_house_size), max(lim_house_size), 51).';
house_price = house_size.^3 + house_size.^2 + 2*house_size + 3 + randn(size(house_size))*20;
disp(table(house_size, house_price)) %[output:0007ea23]
%[text] Let's plot the price vs size data:
figure; %[output:2aa101a7]
plot(house_size, house_price, 'o', 'LineWidth', 1) %[output:2aa101a7]
xlabel('Size (sq. feet)') %[output:2aa101a7]
ylabel('Price ($)') %[output:2aa101a7]
grid on %[output:2aa101a7]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_98e9] ### Linear Regression
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1f46] #### Underfitting
%[text] We can fit a linear function $f\_{w,b}(x) = w\_1x + b $ to this data, where $w\_1$and $b$ are the parameters that will be found from the data itself. The input parameter (size of a house) and corresponding price are denoted by $x$ and $f\_{w,b}(x)$, respectively.
% Note that we will be using polyfit function to obtain underfit (1st
% degree), goodfit (3rd degree), and overfit (19th degree) models. Polyfit
% returns the coefficients as follows:
% [p,~,mu] = polyfit(x,y,n), performs centering and scaling to numerical
% properties of both polynomial and the fitting algorithm.
% The parameter p is the polynomial coefficients for the best fit, p(x) =
% p1*x^n + p2*x^(n-1) + ... + pn+1 The parameters mu(1) is the mean(x) and
% mu(2) is the std(x). Using these values, polyfit centers x at zero and
% scales it to have unit standard deviation.
load('polyfit_1.mat')
x1_val = (house_size-mu(1))./mu(2);
yval = (p(1).*x1_val) + p(2);

figure; %[output:188a1145]
plot(house_size, house_price, 'o', 'LineWidth', 1) %[output:188a1145]
hold on %[output:188a1145]
plot(house_size, yval, '-', 'LineWidth', 1.5) %[output:188a1145]
xlabel('Size (sq. feet)') %[output:188a1145]
ylabel('Price ($)') %[output:188a1145]
grid on %[output:188a1145]
hold off %[output:188a1145]
legend('Data', 'Linear Fit', 'Location','northwest') %[output:188a1145]
title("Underfitting") %[output:188a1145]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_22ae] As we can see from the above fitting this is not a good model to represent the relationship between the size and the price of the houses in the dataset. 
%[text] It is clear from the data that as the size increases the rate of increase in the house price decreases up to a point and it increases again. <u>Hence, we can say that this algorithm cannot fit the training data very well.</u> The technical way of expressing this is that "**the model is underfitting the training data (or the algorithm has a high bias)**". A way to think about bias in machine learning is to imagine that the learning algorithm has a very strong preconception that housing prices will be a completely linear function of house size.
%[text] Let's look at another scenario that how the model could fit the data in the following subsection.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_771a] #### Good Fitting
%[text] Let's use a quadratic function $f(x) = w\_1x + w\_2x^2 + w\_3x^3 +b $ to fit the data, where the $w\_1$, $w\_2$, and $b$ are the parameters to be found. Note that this cubic function has two input features $x$, $x^2$, and $x^3$.
load('polyfit_3.mat')
x1_val = (house_size-mu(1))./mu(2);
yval = (p(1).*x1_val.^3) + (p(2).*x1_val.^2) + (p(3).*x1_val) + p(4);

figure; %[output:1fc9954a]
plot(house_size, house_price, 'o', 'LineWidth', 1) %[output:1fc9954a]
hold on %[output:1fc9954a]
plot(house_size, yval, '-', 'LineWidth', 1.5) %[output:1fc9954a]
xlabel('Size (sq. feet)') %[output:1fc9954a]
ylabel('Price ($)') %[output:1fc9954a]
grid on %[output:1fc9954a]
hold off %[output:1fc9954a]
legend('Data', 'Cubic Fit', 'Location','northwest') %[output:1fc9954a]
title("Goodfitting") %[output:1fc9954a]
%[text] From the above figure, we can see that our model fits the data (training set) pretty well. If you were to guess the price of a house not included in this training dataset, this model would probably perform well on that data, meaning it can understand and represent the governing rules captured in the training data. Note that the model doesn't fit the data samples perfectly, but it is quite close.
%[text] Now, lets look at the other extreme case.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9d5f] #### Overfitting
%[text] What if we tried to fit the fourth order polynomial function $f(x) = w\_1x + w\_2x^2 + \\cdots + w\_{18} x^{18} + w\_{19} x^{19} + b$ into our dataset?
load('polyfit_19.mat')
x1_val = (house_size-mu(1))./mu(2);
xval_mtx = x1_val.^(19:-1:0);

figure; %[output:83f5137b]
plot(house_size, house_price, 'o', 'LineWidth', 1) %[output:83f5137b]
hold on %[output:83f5137b]
plot(house_size, sum(p.*xval_mtx,2), '-', 'LineWidth', 1.5) %[output:83f5137b]
xlabel('Size (sq. feet)') %[output:83f5137b]
ylabel('Price ($)') %[output:83f5137b]
grid on %[output:83f5137b]
hold off %[output:83f5137b]
legend('Data', '19^{th}-order Fit', 'Location','northwest') %[output:83f5137b]
title("Overfitting") %[output:83f5137b]
%[text] As we can see from the figure above, it seems like it is doing an extremely good job modeling the training data. Also, we can say that the cost function values are smaller than the goodfit since the model wraps the training data points better than the goodfit curve. The model above also has some interesting inconsistencies for instance even though some data points have larger house size values, their value is less than the other houses with smaller sizes, which might indicate that this particular model might not be a best model to predict the house prices based on the sizes. In technical terms, we can say that this model **overfits** the data, which means that the model represents/fits the training data almost **too well**. Another problem is that the model might not do well under the test set showing lower generalization capability to capture the underlying trends. In other words, we can say that this model has a **high variance**.
%[text] The problem here is that the model tries to fit the training data really hard, which results very low performance if the test data is even slightly different than the training data. If two separate machine learning engineers tries to fit this 19-th order polynomial curve, they might  end up having very different curves. This high variability suggest the high variance of the overfitting scenarios. Our main target in solving machine learning problems is to find a model that exactly fits the underlying trends ("Goodfit") based on the training and test datasets (**low bias and low variance**).
%[text] So far we've investigated underfitting, overfitting, and goodfitting for linear regression. Let's now investigate a classification example with two features $x\_1$ and $x\_2$.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_049a] ### Logistic Regression
%[text] Let's load the Fisher's Iris dataset and filter it for only petal length and petal width,
load('fisheriris_versicolor_virginica_petal.mat')
%[text] Let's min-max normalize the data to make the visualization simpler,
% min-max normalization
f = figure; %[output:14a3c35a]
tiledlayout(2, 2, "TileSpacing", "compact") %[output:14a3c35a]
nexttile %[output:14a3c35a]
histogram(X_train(:,1), 15) %[output:14a3c35a]
xlabel("x_1") %[output:14a3c35a]
ylabel("Relative frequency") %[output:14a3c35a]
title("Before normalization") %[output:14a3c35a]

nexttile %[output:14a3c35a]
histogram(X_train(:,2), 15) %[output:14a3c35a]
xlabel("x_2") %[output:14a3c35a]
ylabel("Relative frequency") %[output:14a3c35a]
title("Before normalization") %[output:14a3c35a]

X_train = normalizeFeatures(X_train, method="min-max");

nexttile %[output:14a3c35a]
histogram(X_train(:,1), 15) %[output:14a3c35a]
xlabel("x_1") %[output:14a3c35a]
ylabel("Relative frequency") %[output:14a3c35a]
title("After normalization") %[output:14a3c35a]

nexttile %[output:14a3c35a]
histogram(X_train(:,2), 15) %[output:14a3c35a]
xlabel("x_2") %[output:14a3c35a]
ylabel("Relative frequency") %[output:14a3c35a]
title("After normalization") %[output:14a3c35a]

f.Position = [0 0 800 400]; %[output:14a3c35a]
%[text] Let's plot the data,
figure; %[output:32e01d64]
hBinaryScatter(X_train, y_train, {'o', 'x'}, {co(1,:), co(2,:)}, 8); %[output:32e01d64]
xlabel('x_1 (petal length)') %[output:32e01d64]
ylabel('x_2 (petal width)') %[output:32e01d64]
legend([repmat("",1,(length(X_train)/2)-1), "0", repmat("",1,(length(X_train)/2)-1), "1"], "Location", "best") %[output:32e01d64]
%[text] where the O's represent the petal length and width for Versicolor and X's represent Virginica. Our goal is to classify a given sample based on its petal length and width to determine its species.
%[text] - Our goal is to find a function that would take the petal length and width of the sample, $\\mathbf{x} = (x\_1, x\_2)$, as an input and outputs a probability of the sample being a member of X (Virginica), $\\hat{y} = P(y=1\~|\~\\mathbf{x})$. \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_72b5] #### Logistic Regression Refresher and Visualise the Decision Regions of a Classifier
%[text] Logistic regression binary classification model takes the input features vector, $\\mathbf{x}^{(i)} = \[x\_1^{(i)}\~x\_2^{(i)}\~\\cdots\\ x\_{j}^{(i)}\]$, and returns the probability value $\\hat{y}^{(i)} = f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}^{(i)})$ that represents the probability, $P(y^{(i)}=1 \~|\~ \\mathbf{x^{(i)}})$, by finding the set of parameters, $\\mathbf{w} = \[w\_1\~w\_2\~\\cdots\~w\_j\]^{\\text{T}}$ and $b$, to minimize the difference between $y^{(i)}$ (true label) and $\\hat{y}^{(i)}$ (estimated label).
%[text] Similar to the linear regression, weighted and biased sum of the input features could be given by,
%[text] $z = \\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\\mathbf{w} + b = w\_1x^{(i)}\_1 +  w\_2x^{(i)}\_2 + \\cdots +  w\_jx^{(i)}\_j + b$
%[text] Also, the relationship between the input features and the predicted logit or log-odds ($z$) is as follows:
%[text] $z = \\text{logit}(\\hat{y}^{(i)})= \\ln \\frac{P(y^{(i)}=1 \~|\~ \\mathbf{x}^{(i)})}{1-P(y^{(i)}=1 \~|\~ \\mathbf{x}^{(i)})} = \\ln \\frac{\\hat{y}^{(i)}}{1-\\hat{y}^{(i)}}$,
%[text] where the definition of logit or log-odds is given by $\\text{logit} (p) = \\ln \\frac{p}{1-p} \\quad \\text{for } p\\in(0,1)$. Note that the logit function is the inverse of the standard logistic function (sigmoid). Also note that the superscripts will be dropped for brevity. We can arrange the above expression as follows:
%[text] $e^{z} = \\frac{\\hat{y}}{1-\\hat{y}} \\quad \\rightarrow \\quad  e^{z} - e^{z}\\hat{y} = \\hat{y} \\quad \\rightarrow \\quad \\hat{y}\\left( 1 + e^{z} \\right) = e^{z}$
%[text] The expression above will directly indicate that $\\hat{y} = \\text{logit}(\\hat{y}) =  \\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}$. Note that the relationship between the input features ($x\_j \~\~ \\text{for}  \~\~ j\\in\\{ 1,2,\\cdots, n \\}$) and the log-odds ($z$) is linear.
%[text] In particular, for a two-dimensional problem, the log-odds become,
%[text] $z = w\_1x\_1 + w\_2x\_2 + b$.
%[text] - It is sometimes useful to be able to visualize the the boundary line dividing the input space in which points are classified as belonging to the class of interest, $P(y=1 \~ | \~ \\mathbf{x}^{(i)})$, from that space in which points do not.
%[text] - This could be achieved by calculating the prediction associated with $\\hat{y}$ for a mesh/surface $(x\_1, x\_2)$ points and plotting contour plot. \
%[text] We know that the classification/decision making in logistic regression works as follows:
%[text] $y = \n\\left\\{\n    \\begin{array}{lr}\n        1, & \\text{if } \\hat{y} \\geq 0.5  \\\\\n        0, & \\text{if } \\hat{y} \< 0.5\n    \\end{array}$ , 
%[text] where$\\hat{y} = \\text{logit}(\\hat{y}) =  \\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}} = \\frac{1}{1+e^{-(w\_1x\_1+w\_2x\_2+b)}}$
%[text] Let's brute force the mesh $(x\_1, x\_2)$ for the parameterization of $w\_1=1$, $w\_2 = 1$, and $b = 0$.
w1 = 3;
w2 = 1.5;
b = 2;

[x1, x2] = meshgrid(linspace(-1, 1, 50));
z = x1.*w1 + x2.*w2 + b;
y_hat = sigma(z);

figure; %[output:7f5fd07d]
surf(x1, x2, y_hat, 'FaceAlpha', 0.7); %[output:7f5fd07d]
hold on %[output:7f5fd07d]
surf(x1, x2, 0.5.*ones(size(y_hat)), 'FaceAlpha', 0.7) %[output:7f5fd07d]
hold off %[output:7f5fd07d]
xlabel('x_1') %[output:7f5fd07d]
ylabel('x_2') %[output:7f5fd07d]
title('Mesh/Surface Approach (x_1, x_2)') %[output:7f5fd07d]
legend('$\hat{y}$ surface', '$\hat{y}= 0.5$', 'Interpreter', 'latex'); %[output:7f5fd07d]
view(3); camlight; axis vis3d %[output:7f5fd07d]
%[text] Let's run the stochastic gradient descent (SGD) algorithm on this logistic regression problem and visualize the decision boundary.
w_init = [0; 0]; % zero initialization
b_init = 0; % zero initialization
alpha = 0.1;
numIters = 10000;
[J_vec, w_vec, b] = hLogisticRegression(X_train, y_train, w_init, b_init, LearnRate=alpha, MaxIter=numIters); %[output:1b127bca]
figure; %[output:5e442530]
plot(1:length(J_vec), J_vec) %[output:5e442530]
xlabel('Iterations') %[output:5e442530]
ylabel('Cost (J)') %[output:5e442530]
grid on %[output:5e442530]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6470] #### Alternative Way to Visualize 2D Decision Boundary
%[text] Alternatively, one can think of decision boundary as the line $x\_2 = mx\_1 + c$, being defined by the points for which $\\hat{y} = 0.5$ or $z = 0$. We can find the line intercept $c$ and slope $m$ terms as follows: 
%[text] - For $x\_1 = 0$, we have $x\_2 = c$ (the $x\_2$ axis intercept) and$z = 0  = 0\\cdot w\_1 + c\\cdot w\_2 + b \\quad \\rightarrow \\quad c = -b / w\_2$.
%[text] - For the slope, we will need two distinctive points on the decision boundary, $(x\_1^a, x\_2^a)$ and $(x\_1^b, x\_2^b)$, so that $m = (x\_2^b- x\_2^a) / (x\_1^b - x\_1^a)$. We know that $z = 0$ for any point on the decision boundary. Therefore, $0 = x\_1^a w\_1 + x\_2^aw\_2 + b$ and $0 = x\_1^b w\_1 + x\_2^b w\_2 + b$, which also means that $w\_1x\_1^b + w\_2x\_2^b + b - \\left(  w\_1x\_1^a + w\_2x\_2^a + b \\right) = 0$. If we arrange the expression, $w\_1x\_1^b - w\_1x\_1^a+ w\_2x\_2^b - w\_2x\_2^a = 0 \~ \\rightarrow \~\nw\_1 \\left( x\_1^b - x\_1^a \\right) + w\_2\\left( x\_2^b - x\_2^a \\right) = 0$. Thus, $\\left( x\_2^b-x\_2^a \\right)/ \\left(x\_1^b-x\_1^a\\right) = -w\_1/w\_2$, which is equal to $m$. \
%[text] Since we know the slope and the intercept, we can get the final decision boundary line as, $x\_2 = -\\frac{w\_1}{w\_2}x\_1 - \\frac{b}{w\_2}$.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_80f8] **A simpler expression:** In a simpler way, we can derive the above expression as follows:
%[text] We have the following weighted sum (or logit of the output probabilities): $w\_1x\_1 + w\_2 x\_2 = 0$, where we know that this expression holds on the entire decision boundary line and we want to plot the relationship between $x\_1$ and $x\_2$. Let's fix one of the parameters $x\_2 = -\\frac{w\_1}{w\_2}x\_1 - \\frac{b}{w\_2}$ , if we treat $x\_1$ as the independent parameter and plot the results, we should be able to obtain the decision boundary.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0c61] #### Underfitting
%[text] Based on the dataset we've investigated above, we can try to fit a simple logistic model to the given data, $z = w\_1x\_1 + w\_2x\_2 + b$, where $f\_{\\mathbf{w},b}(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sigma(z) = g(z)$, where $\\sigma(\\cdot)$ and $g(\\cdot)$ represents the sigmoid function.
figure; %[output:7cc0f469]
hBinaryScatter(X_train, y_train, {'o', 'x'}, {co(1,:), co(2,:)}, 8); %[output:7cc0f469]
xlabel('x_1 (petal length)') %[output:7cc0f469]
ylabel('x_2 (petal width)') %[output:7cc0f469]
hold on %[output:7cc0f469]
% decision boundary, w(1)*x1 + w(2)*x2 + b = 0
% x2 = -b/w(2) - w(1)/w(2)*x1
x1 = linspace(min(X_train, [], "all"), max(X_train, [], "all"), 50);
plot(x1, (-b/w_vec(2))-(w_vec(1)/w_vec(2)).*x1, 'Color', co(3,:), 'LineWidth', 1.5) %[output:7cc0f469]
hold off %[output:7cc0f469]
title("Decision boundary line found by the SGD algorithm") %[output:7cc0f469]
%[text] The yellow curve is our decision boundary where the under the curve the samples are classified as O and above the curve the samples are classified as X. At a first glance we can say that this curve doesn't do a terrible job but it is not a good fit to the data. However, the model shows a high bias which means that it **underfits** the training data.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1b94] #### Goodfitting
%[text] Now, let's look at a more complex decision boundary, such as $z = w\_1x\_1 + w\_2x\_2 + w\_3x\_1^2 + w\_4x\_2^2 + w\_5x\_1x\_2 + b$. Let's apply SGD algorithm first and use brute force the surface to plot the decision boundary,
w_init = [0; 0; 0; 0; 0]; % zero initialization
b_init = 0; % zero initialization
alpha = 0.1;
numIters = 1e5;
% Design the feature matrix such that [x1 x2 x1^2 x^2 x1*x2]
X_train_4 = [X_train(:,1) X_train(:,2) X_train(:,1).^2 X_train(:,2).^2 X_train(:,1).*X_train(:,2)];
[J_vec_1, w_vec_1, b_1] = hLogisticRegression(X_train_4, y_train, w_init, b_init, LearnRate=alpha, MaxIter=numIters, DispFreq=10000); %[output:1b3cc057]
figure; %[output:2ee86397]
plot(1:length(J_vec_1), J_vec_1) %[output:2ee86397]
xlabel('Iterations') %[output:2ee86397]
ylabel('Cost (J)') %[output:2ee86397]
grid on %[output:2ee86397]
%[text] **Interactive Decision Boundary Visualization**
w_vec_1(1) = 7.7251; %[control:slider:8acd]{"position":[14,20]}
w_vec_1(2) = 7.1587; %[control:slider:4c2a]{"position":[14,20]}
b_1        = 1.2833; %[control:slider:0f9d]{"position":[14,20]}
f = figure; %[output:6632b181]
plotDecisionBoundarySurface(X_train, y_train, w_vec_1, b_1); %[output:6632b181]
f.Position = [0 0 850 600]; %[output:6632b181]
%[text] We can see from the plots above that the this ellipse-like decision boundary fits the data pretty good even though it doesn't perfectly classify every single sample correctly. The above "just-right" decision boundary will be able to generalize the new patient data the best, where the model is able to capture the underlying relationship between two classes and draw a decision boundary.
%%
%[text] Now, let's try to only visualize the $z = 0$ or $\\sigma(z) = 0.5$ curves,
w_vec_1(1)  = 7.7251; %[control:slider:74a5]{"position":[15,21]}
w_vec_1(2)  = 7.1587; %[control:slider:4478]{"position":[15,21]}
b_1         = 1.2833; %[control:slider:503a]{"position":[15,21]}
tolerance_1 = 0.11; %[control:slider:39a0]{"position":[15,19]}
f = figure; %[output:3cdc10bc]
plotDecisionBoundaryLine(X_train, y_train, w_vec_1, b_1, tolerance_1); %[output:3cdc10bc]
f.Position = [0 0 800 400]; %[output:3cdc10bc]
view([0 90]) %[output:3cdc10bc]
%%
%[text] Let's also try to visualize the contour plot between $z$-axis differences to find an other way to visualize the decision boundary/regions,
w_vec_1(1)  = 7.7251; %[control:slider:5f38]{"position":[15,21]}
w_vec_1(2)  = 7.1587; %[control:slider:20c8]{"position":[15,21]}
b_1         = 1.2833; %[control:slider:8535]{"position":[15,21]}
f = figure; %[output:211d4d69]
plotDecisionBoundaryLineUsingZDiff(X_train, y_train, w_vec_1, b_1); %[output:211d4d69]
f.Position = [0 0 800 400]; %[output:211d4d69]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_80c3] #### Overfitting
%[text] Finally, at the other extreme we can try to fit a very high order polynomial such as 
%[text] $z = w\_1x\_1^3 + w\_2x\_1^3x\_2 + w\_3x\_1^3x\_2^2 + w\_4x\_1^3x\_2^3 + w\_5x\_1^2 + w\_6x\_1^2x\_2 \\\\\n+ w\_7x\_1^2x\_2^2 + w\_8x\_1^2x\_2^3\n+ w\_9x\_1 + w\_{10}x\_1x\_2 + w\_{11}x\_1x\_2^2\n+ w\_{12}x\_1x\_2^3 + w\_{13}x\_2 + w\_{14}x\_2^2 + w\_{15}x\_2^3 + b$
%[text] How can we automate the above structure?
% Design the feature matrix such that
% | x1^(2) | x1^(2)*x2 | x1^(2)*x2^(2) | x1 | x1*x2 | x1*x2^(2) | x2 | x2^(2) |
X_train_test = [X_train(:,1).^2, X_train(:,1).^2.*X_train(:,2), X_train(:,1).^2.*X_train(:,2).^2,...
                X_train(:,1), X_train(:,1).*X_train(:,2), X_train(:,1).*X_train(:,2).^2, X_train(:,2), X_train(:,2).^2];
X_train_2 = polynomialFeatureExpansion2D(X_train, n=2); %[output:85f4a4b4]
isequal(X_train_test, X_train_2) %[output:67804501]
%[text] Let's use above polynomial expansion of the training data with SGD,
norder = 4; % nth order polynomial expansion

% 4-th order polynomial expansion of the parameters x_1 and x_2
X_train_4 = polynomialFeatureExpansion2D(X_train, n=norder); %[output:0eb0bb93]
w_init = zeros(size(X_train_4, 2), 1); % zero initialization
b_init = 0; % zero initialization
alpha = 0.1; % learning rate
numIters = 1e6; % number of iterations
[J_vec_4, w_vec_4, b_4] = hLogisticRegression(X_train_4, y_train, w_init, b_init, LearnRate=alpha, MaxIter=numIters, DispFreq=1e5); %[output:1df21361]
% Calculate the decision boundary contour
[x1_loc, x2_loc, z_loc] = decisionBoundaryLineUsingZDiff(X_train_4, w_vec_4, b_4, n=norder, func="sigmoid"); %[output:5bf5afad]
% Plot the decision boundary contour
figure; %[output:2f220375]
hBinaryScatter3D(X_train, y_train, {co(1,:), co(2,:)}, 6); %[output:2f220375]
hold on %[output:2f220375]
grid on %[output:2f220375]
line(x1_loc, x2_loc, z_loc, 'Color', co(3,:), 'LineWidth', 2); %[output:2f220375]
hold off %[output:2f220375]
xlabel("x_1") %[output:2f220375]
ylabel("x_2") %[output:2f220375]
view([0 90]) %[output:2f220375]
xlim([-1 1]) %[output:2f220375]
ylim([-1 1]) %[output:2f220375]
axis square %[output:2f220375]
title("Decision Boundary (Overfit), \sigma(z)") %[output:2f220375]
%[text] We can see from the above figure that the model is trying really hard to fit the contours around individual samples to **find a decision boundary that would fit our training data perfectly.** Having all these high order polynomial features (after the polynomial expansion of the training data) allows the model to choose a very (overly) complex decision boundary. If the features correspond to petal length and petal width an we are trying to determine whether a given plant is Versicolor (O) or Virginica (X), then this doesn't look like a good model for making predictions since this model shows a ovefitting and high variance, where the model won't be able to generalize well the new examples since it fitted itself to the training data very tightly.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_56bd] ## (Bonus Content)  The Optimal Model for the Logistic Regression Task
%[text] Related to the above investigation we go another step further and try to find an optimal model for a logistic regression task for the training data provided above,
alpha = 0.1; % learning rate
numIters = 1e6; % number of iterations
dispFreq = 1e5; % display frequency
C = orderedcolors("gem12");

f = figure; %[output:1472114c]
hBinaryScatter3D(X_train, y_train, {C(1,:), C(2,:)}, 6); %[output:1472114c]
hold on %[output:1472114c]
for n = 1:2:15 %[output:group:726b0314]
    fprintf('\n n=%d\n', n); %[output:66528086] %[output:4d7c28d6] %[output:084ae656] %[output:96e04c6e] %[output:616b2fcc] %[output:39d47e32] %[output:16642669] %[output:4e589b02]
    % nth order polynomial expansion of the parameters x_1 and x_2
    X_train_n = polynomialFeatureExpansion2D(X_train, n=n); %[output:4543571b] %[output:89d78f3e] %[output:647a90c5] %[output:1b40b8ba] %[output:40185e9a] %[output:69a3494c] %[output:880a4d84] %[output:21e705f0]

    % SGD
    w_init = zeros(size(X_train_n,2),1); % zero initialization
    b_init = 0; % zero initialization
    [J_vec_n, w_vec_n, b_n] = hLogisticRegression(X_train_n, y_train, w_init, b_init, LearnRate=alpha, MaxIter=numIters, DispFreq=dispFreq); %[output:842331dc] %[output:7673c8b1] %[output:37be3da9] %[output:77a99f3f] %[output:9bb1163e] %[output:2a87ad69] %[output:51bcbd8c] %[output:1bc99089]
    fprintf('\n');

    % Calculate the decision boundary contour
    [x1_loc, x2_loc, z_loc] = decisionBoundaryLineUsingZDiff(X_train_n, w_vec_n, b_n, n=n, func="sigmoid"); %[output:0d608ae7] %[output:4caf0e27] %[output:1e4e374f] %[output:48f55bc5] %[output:72614828] %[output:8e7ef095] %[output:03d8ad8f] %[output:427ee727]
    
    % Plot the decision boundary contour    
    line(x1_loc, x2_loc, z_loc, 'Color', C(mod(n-1,12)+1,:), 'LineWidth', 2, 'LineStyle', '-.'); %[output:1472114c]
    
end %[output:group:726b0314]
hold off %[output:1472114c]
grid on %[output:1472114c]
f.Position = [0 0 800 400]; %[output:1472114c]
view([0 90]) %[output:1472114c]
xlabel("x_1") %[output:1472114c]
ylabel("x_2") %[output:1472114c]
view([0 90]) %[output:1472114c]
xlim([-1 1]) %[output:1472114c]
ylim([-1 1]) %[output:1472114c]
axis square %[output:1472114c]
title("Decision Boundary, \sigma(z)") %[output:1472114c]
legend([repmat("",1,length(X_train)) string(1:2:15)]) %[output:1472114c]
